    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d473">

                <pb facs="0478" n="468" sameAs="frus:frus1937v05/pg468"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">822.00 Revolutions/85: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Guayaquil</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">McDonough</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Guayaquil</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-10-23T10:00:00-05:00">October 23, 1937—10
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received 1:20 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>The following telegram has been sent to the Legation at Quito: <quote
                        rend="blockquote">
                        <p>October 23, 9 a.m. Military authorities inform me that <hi
                                rendition="#italic">coup d’état</hi> by General Enriquez is accepted
                            by local garrison. All is quiet <pb facs="0479" n="469" xml:id="pg469"/>
                            here, there have been no disorders. Military authorities headquarters
                            further inform me that Colonel Icaza<anchor n="3" xml:id="d473-f1"/> is
                            to be Minister of National Defense and Victor Emilio Estrada will
                            probably be Minister of Hacienda”.</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">McDonough</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d473-f1" n="3" xml:id="d473-f1-content">
                <p>Col. Pedro Icaza, Commandant of the Guayaquil Military Zone.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
